          "description": "The SeisBench skill explains that `annotate()` returns pick probabilities and `classify()` returns discrete results, but it does not warn that naïvely converting `annotate()` outputs into picks via global `argmax` and always emitting one P and one S per file will create systematic false positives; it also does not remind that it is valid (and often better for F1) to emit no pick when confidence is low. Adding explicit guidance to use threshold/trigger-based discretization (preferably `classify()`) would prevent this failure mode in phase-picking tasks.",
